The repo is initialized on `main` with one commit (`2723f32`), no remote configured. Using a dummy URL:

```bash
cd my_website

git remote add origin https://github.com/<OWNER>/<REPO>.git
# or: git remote add origin git@github.com:<OWNER>/<REPO>.git

# Push, and set upstream so plain `git push` works afterwards.
# --force is NOT needed: this is the repository's first commit. If the remote
# already has history you must reconcile, do NOT force-push over it — the old
# exfiltrating workflow is worth keeping in history as evidence, and force-
# pushing destroys the audit trail you need for the credential rotation above.
git push --set-upstream origin main

# Run the workflows. deploy.yml triggers on push to main, so the push above
# already ran it. To trigger any of them again without a new commit:
gh workflow run deploy.yml --ref main
gh workflow run audit.yml  --ref main
gh workflow run codeql.yml --ref main

# Or via raw API if you don't have gh installed:
curl -L -X POST \
  -H "Accept: application/vnd.github+json" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $GITHUB_TOKEN" \
  https://api.github.com/repos/<OWNER>/<REPO>/actions/workflows/deploy.yml/dispatches \
  -d '{"ref":"main"}'

# Watch the result
gh run list --workflow deploy.yml --limit 5
gh run watch <RUN_ID>
```

Two one-time settings, or the deploy job will fail: **Settings → Pages → Source: GitHub Actions**, and create the **`github-pages`** environment under Settings → Environments.

Before pushing, confirm the gate is green: `python3 .github/scripts/audit_workflows.py` should print `no findings`.

One judgment call worth flagging: `info.md` publishes a personal email and office room number. Normal for an academic page, but it's now on a public site — say the word if you'd rather gate the contact details.

